Here is what I think is going on - the AIA URL on the leaf certificate points to `http://ca.playtechgaming.com/`. That URL returns a zero byte result when downloaded in `CertificateAssetDownloader`

https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/cfd0172f89d237f79c47de51b64d5778c440bbc6/src/libraries/System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates/src/Internal/Cryptography/Pal.Unix/CertificateAssetDownloader.cs#L24-L38 

Passing `byte[0]` to `X509Certificate2.ctor` doesn't throw, you just get a "default" certificate. We never actually touch the certificate to make sure it's real, we just try chain building again with this default cert and a null PAL.

Changing the cert downloader to throw if the certificate is in an invalid state fixes it.

```c#
try
{
    X509Certificate2 cert = new X509Certificate2(data);
    cert.ThrowIfInvalid();
    return cert;
}
catch (CryptographicException)
{
    return null;
}
```
